<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Qt's own template, with three deliberate differences. Compare against
    <qt-android>/src/android/templates/AndroidManifest.xml
  after a Qt bump.

  1. NO "%%INSERT_PERMISSIONS" MARKER, and that is the whole point of this file
     existing. androiddeployqt does not read the code to decide what an app may
     do; it unions the permissions declared by every Qt module it deploys and
     pastes them in at that marker. Qt Network is linked transitively by Qt
     Quick, so a calculator that has never opened a socket was asking for
     INTERNET, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE and
     WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE - measured on the first APK this project ever
     built. Gert, 2026sep06: "Remember to request the required permissions for
     the APK, but make them be requested to the system as they become required.
     For example, only if the user desires to change to a folder outside the
     sandbox to integrate Dropbox, and so on." With the marker gone the union is
     pasted nowhere and the app ships asking for nothing at all.

     Nothing is lost by it. The calculator's own memory lives in the app's
     private folder, which needs no permission; a folder outside it is reached
     through the Storage Access Framework, which grants per folder at the moment
     the user picks one and is not a manifest permission either. The one thing
     that would need a real permission is a legacy /sdcard path, which this app
     deliberately does not use.

     One permission does still appear in the built manifest, and it is not ours
     to remove: androidx.core declares
     "<applicationId>.DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION", a signature
     permission scoped to this app that exists so its own broadcast receivers
     are not exported. It grants nothing to the app, is never shown to the user
     and never prompts. Deleting it with tools:node="remove" would only break
     androidx's own receiver registration.

  2. package, and a launcher icon. Qt's default is org.qtproject.example.<app>,
     which would put this calculator in the same namespace as Qt's examples and
     next to a blank icon in the launcher. The Java in src/ has always been
     dk.geeak.agape48; this makes the package agree with it.

  3. android:screenOrientation is "portrait", which is the dogfood round's
     answer to the question this comment used to leave open. Gert, 2026sep07,
     on the phone: "please keep the Agape48 from rotating when the phone turns
     around. It looks too ugly." A 48GX is a portrait object - the face is 854
     wide for 1438 tall - so landscape can only letterbox it or stretch it, and
     the Android build already stretches to fill, which in landscape would make
     the keys twice as wide as they are tall.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="dk.geeak.agape48"
    android:installLocation="auto"
    android:versionCode="-- %%INSERT_VERSION_CODE%% --"
    android:versionName="-- %%INSERT_VERSION_NAME%% --">
    <!-- %%INSERT_FEATURES -->
    <supports-screens
        android:anyDensity="true"
        android:largeScreens="true"
        android:normalScreens="true"
        android:smallScreens="true" />
    <application
        android:name="org.qtproject.qt.android.bindings.QtApplication"
        android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
        android:label="-- %%INSERT_APP_NAME%% --"
        android:icon="@drawable/icon"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:fullBackupOnly="false">
        <activity
            android:name="org.qtproject.qt.android.bindings.QtActivity"
            android:configChanges="orientation|uiMode|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|layoutDirection|locale|fontScale|keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation|mcc|mnc|density"
            android:label="-- %%INSERT_APP_NAME%% --"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.lib_name"
                android:value="-- %%INSERT_APP_LIB_NAME%% --" />

            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.arguments"
                android:value="-- %%INSERT_APP_ARGUMENTS%% --" />
        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.qtprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/qtprovider_paths"/>
        </provider>
    </application>
</manifest>
